/**
 * The app, in Polish.
 *
 * Keyed on the ENGLISH source string, exactly as the German and French tables
 * are, because that is what ui() is handed at the call site. One character out
 * and the lookup misses in silence: the app draws the English and nothing
 * anywhere reports a problem. So this file is generated from the German
 * table's own keys rather than typed, and check-polish-interface fails the
 * build if the two ever hold different keys.
 *
 * GENDER. Polish marks the speaker's gender in the past tense, so a literal
 * 'you wrote' has to pick one. An app does not know who is reading it, so the
 * second-person past is avoided throughout: impersonal forms ('zapisano',
 * 'przerwano') or the present tense say the same thing without guessing.
 * check-polish-interface fails on -łaś and -łeś for that reason.
 *
 * The last seven entries are German, not English — those are the fallbacks
 * uiOr() is given at its call sites, which have no English key to be found
 * under.
 */
